    "rationale": "Implements the joint selection (groups f833b57a373d8262a384 and 8525af72c631b94e3dd0) at the single shared c10d.allreduce_.default boundary that jointly owns all 13 bucket collectives of the step. Measured basis: non-NCCL compute kernels total ~34.0 ms for all 4 micro-batches while the 13 bucket allreduces cost ~61.8 ms cleanly (collective_calibration medians 3.52 ms x 12 + 19.50 ms, ~8.05 GB/s logical), communication is essentially exposed at the step tail (only 8.49 ms of compute remains after the first collective kernel starts), and the wire path is NCCL ring over SHM (peer_access 0 both directions, no NVLink, 2 channels) with telemetry pcie.tx ~7.3 GB/s / pcie.rx ~8.5 GB/s during the comm window. The replacement swaps the dispatch at both call IDs for a chunked one-shot two-rank exchange on the same worker-initialized NCCL process group: each flat contiguous float32 bucket view is split into halves; grouped isend/irecv (ncclGroup, both directions concurrently, unique monotonic tags per invocation/round) exchanges the halves; one local elementwise add reduces the kept half; a second grouped exchange swaps the reduced halves back into the gradient_as_bucket_view buffer; the callable returns (tensors, Work) with the final irecv handle boxed via Work.boxed, preserving async_op=True semantics at this op's exact ABI. Wire volume per rank is unchanged (S bytes per direction, the information-theoretic minimum for an exact allreduce); the hypothesized gain is transport efficiency of grouped bulk P2P transfers versus the observed ring schedule and remains a measurement question exactly as the selection states: if ~8 GB/s is this GPU pair's link ceiling, step_ms will not improve and the candidate must be rejected on paired measurement; expected benefit is not acceptance. Numerics: DDP's world-size division happens before this boundary and is untouched; every output element is a single commutative two-addend fp32 sum computed on exactly one rank and then copied to the peer, so cross-rank gradients are bitwise identical by construction (exact_gradient_replica_check atol=0.0/rtol=0.0 holds structurally) and equal the ring SUM of the same addends, keeping the full-global-batch gradient reference check and the post-step parameter check unchanged; losses are unaffected. Guards are rank-symmetric (single 1-D contiguous float32 CUDA tensor >= 2M elements, NCCL process group with exactly ranks [0,1], ReduceOp.SUM positively identified via the ScriptObject's op() accessor, sparse_indices None); any other case falls back to the parent op before anything is enqueued, with distinct route labels (guard_*/setup_*/error_* for predicate, setup and pre-enqueue exception categories). After the first enqueue no parent fallback is taken (that would double-reduce the bucket); failures propagate loudly instead. Per-direction P2P op order is identical on both ranks and unique tags make matching explicit even if bucket issue order skews. No inputs/previous-feedback.txt and no inputs/previous-candidate/ exist for this attempt, so there is no prior attempt feedback to incorporate. The diagnostic communication goal in workloads.json (max_rank_nccl_kernel_sum_ms with the 1.05x step_ms constraint) is not part of the user-requested objective; replacing AllReduce kernels with Send/Recv kernels changes what that diagnostic counts without touching step timing semantics.",
    "setup_work": {
      "build_time": "No model/parameter transforms, no CUDA Graph capture, no new process groups, no environment changes, no comm hooks. build() only reads context['parameters']['chunks'] (default 8, clamped to [1,64]) and creates two replacement closures over context['references'] plus a per-device scratch cache shared by both call IDs.",
      "runtime_lazy": "The first supported invocation allocates one persistent float32 scratch of numel/2 elements sized for the largest observed bucket (19,692,672 elements = 75.0 MiB for the 39,385,344-element embedding bucket; the 7,087,872-element buckets reuse a prefix slice). The allocation happens once per worker during the first (warmup) invocation and the buffer is retained for the process lifetime; it is never freed, so the caching allocator cannot race multi-stream reuse. First-use NCCL P2P path warmup also lands in warmup invocations.",
      "per_call_work": "Two grouped batch_isend_irecv calls (sends plus recvs in one ncclGroup each), one elementwise add over half the bucket, and Work.wait() event syncs. The CPU never blocks: NCCL wait() performs event-based stream synchronization, and round-2 sends are ordered after the adds by ProcessGroupNCCL's enqueue-time current-stream sync.",
      "memory_impact": "Steady state retains +75.0 MiB allocated scratch, visible in framework.peak_allocated_bytes and lifecycle counters; no per-invocation growth; no graphs or retained graph pools. This is a documented setup/retained allocation, not a general physical-memory saving. Per-call transient Python objects are O(chunks)."
